-## Open Design Choice: Concurrent Visit Edits
+## Concurrency Policy: Whole-Visit Revisions
 
-OPEN: select the concurrency policy before completing this node's public contract.
+This node adopts whole-visit revisions. Each submission declares a base revision ID (null for the first creation). When multiple edits derive from the same base revision and affect the same visit, they create competing revisions. A coordinator must explicitly select one, merge them, or request clarification; no automatic combination occurs.
 
-Two approaches remain credible:
+**Rationale**: Preserves the worker's submitted visit as a unit and avoids combining edits across scientific relationships the system cannot understand. The review burden falls on the human coordinators (who already reconcile field notes), not on auto-merge rules.
 
-- Whole-visit revisions: each edit names its starting revision. Concurrent edits produce competing complete revisions for a coordinator to resolve. This preserves the worker's submitted visit as a unit and needs relatively little merge logic, but even edits to unrelated fields create review work. Tablet-sync would retain base revision IDs and display pending competing revisions. Review-export would need a revision comparison workflow and exclude unresolved visits from its export set.
-- Field-aware three-way merge: each edit supplies its base state and changes. Disjoint field changes combine automatically; overlapping changes require review. This reduces review volume and gets more visits ready for export, but requires maintained rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries. Automatically combined fields can also cross scientific relationships the rules do not understand. Tablet-sync would retain base snapshots and send structured changes. Review-export would need field-level provenance and a review view for overlapping changes.
+**Downstream impact**: Tablet-sync retains base revision IDs with local drafts and signals pending competing revisions to the field worker. Review-export surfaces revision conflicts, requires coordinator resolution before export, and excludes unresolved visits from the CSV.
 
-The choice trades review workload and submission size against implementation burden and preservation of whole-visit intent. Neither approach may discard original submissions. Field-aware merging is not ruled out by a requirement that every combined record receive manual approval.
+## Submission and Resolution Workflow
 
-## Downstream Contract Work
+**Submission envelope:**
+- `visit_id` (UUID, client-generated)
+- `base_revision_id` (revision ID or null for creation)
+- `author_id` (authenticated worker)
+- `idempotency_key` (UUID, set by client)
+- `submitted_at` (server-assigned receipt time)
+- `fields` (visit payload: plot_id, observation_date, observer_names, species_cover, notes)
 
-After selection, define the submission envelope, current-state response, unresolved-work representation, and resolution operation here. Tablet-sync will size its local storage and retry state machine around that envelope. Review-export will size its coordinator interface and export eligibility rules around the unresolved-work representation. Both must link back to this node rather than invent their own concurrency semantics.
+**Current-state response** includes:
+- `visit_id` and the latest (or coordinator-selected) revision ID
+- The merged/selected visit state
+- A presence field: `has_unresolved_revisions` (boolean) — true if competing revisions exist that no coordinator has resolved
+
+**Unresolved-work representation**: A visit with unresolved revisions stores the coordinator-facing metadata:
+- All competing revision IDs and their submission metadata (author, receipt time, base_revision_id)
+- The payload of each competing revision side-by-side for comparison
+- No resolution yet assigned
+
+**Resolution operation**: A coordinator posts a resolution identifying:
+- `visit_id`
+- `resolution_type`: one of `accept_revision`, `merge_revisions`, `request_clarification`
+- For `accept_revision`: the chosen `revision_id`
+- For `merge_revisions`: the merged `fields` payload (the coordinator manually combines the edits)
+- For `request_clarification`: a `note_to_requester` sent back to the field worker
+- The resolving coordinator's `id` and `resolved_at` (server-assigned)
+
+Original submissions are never deleted; resolutions and selected revisions are appended to the visit's history.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Selection | Rationale | Alternatives Considered |
+|----------|-----------|-----------|------------------------|
+| Concurrent visit edit handling | Whole-visit revisions | Prevents silent combination of unrelated field edits and preserves worker intent as a unit. Coordinators are already reconciling field observations; they can weight competing revisions in context. | Field-aware three-way merge (more automatic reconciliation, larger review capacity, but risks combining edits without understanding underlying scientific relationships) |
+| Base revision tracking | Client submits base_revision_id | Allows the server to detect true concurrency and preserve unmerged branches. | Server infers concurrency from timing (less precise; cannot distinguish intentional edits from offline delays) |
+| Latest-state retrieval | Return has_unresolved_revisions flag | Allows tablets and export logic to show reviewability status without expensive computation. | Compute unresolved status on demand (higher latency; blocks export decision) |
